#: slug-sync.php:1635
msgid "Step one"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1635
msgid "Choose the content to check"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1636
msgid "Only the selected content type is processed. Attachments and product variations are never included."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1637
msgid "Content type"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1646
msgid "This content type nests inside a parent, so plan redirects yourself."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1647
msgid "WordPress only redirects an old URL by itself for content that does not nest, such as posts and products. It does not do so for pages, whatever tool changes the slug, so import the redirect report into a redirect plugin after applying."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1648
msgid "A nested URL also contains its parents' slugs. Renaming a parent therefore changes the URL of everything beneath it, and those child URLs are not in the reports, which list only items whose own slug changed. Check what sits under anything you rename."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1653
msgid "Step two"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1653
msgid "Choose what to do"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1654
msgid "Start with a preview. Apply uses the same matching rules but saves the proposed slugs."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1659
msgid "Create a preview"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1659
msgid "Recommended first"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1660
msgid "Reads the selected content and creates reports. No slugs, URLs, posts, or products are changed."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1666
msgid "Apply the slug changes"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1667
msgid "Changes matching slugs in batches and records reports for downloads and undo. Use this after reviewing a preview."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1672
msgid "Apply will change live URLs."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1673
msgid "Take a database backup first. Existing indexed URLs are recorded for redirection, and a redirect CSV is created as a second layer."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1678
msgid "Step three"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1678
msgid "Choose how Apply saves each slug"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1684
#: slug-sync.php:2082
msgid "Quiet update"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1684
msgid "Recommended"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1685
msgid "Best for most sites and large stores. Changes only the URL slug, keeps the existing modified date, and avoids triggering save automations, webhooks, or integration syncs for every item. Redirect protections and reports are still created."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1691
#: slug-sync.php:2082
msgid "Standard WordPress update"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1692
msgid "Saves every item through WordPress normally. This updates its modified date and runs plugins, webhooks, and automations connected to saving. Choose this only when another integration must react to each slug change; it can be much slower."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1699
msgid "Step four"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1699
msgid "Choose what is included"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1700
msgid "By default, only published items whose slug clearly differs from the title are included. Leave these unchecked unless you need the broader scope described."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1705
msgid "Also include unpublished items"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1706
msgid "Includes drafts, pending-review items, and private items. Leave off if you only want to change URLs that visitors can currently access."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1712
msgid "Recheck numbered slugs"
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1713
msgid "Includes slugs that already match the title except for a number. These are skipped by default because the number often marks a duplicate title. WordPress may add a number again when it is needed to keep the URL unique."
msgstr ""

#: slug-sync.php:1714
msgid "Example: blue-cotton-shirt-2"
msgstr ""

